Derive an exact finite production schedule and its optimality boundary (@engineering-production-scheduling: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Account-for-rework-yield-and-resource-load-without-creating-items.lingua#r_009A472FF42DC85607FFCD97B2") { r_00336206494F38CCB92CFFA910
A state is the tuple of job phases. Time t and the fixed future availability calendar are also part of the scheduling problem. From each state enumerate one eligible A job or idle and one eligible B job or idle, advance the selected phases, and reject destinations exceeding either waiting capacity. Because the jobs' next operations alternate, the same job cannot be eligible on both machines. Idling is retained; compulsory busy work would change the feasible set.

To compare schedules, first maximize accepted departures by the horizon, then minimize scrap departures, then minimize final WIP, then minimize accumulated item-time inside the system. This is an explicit lexicographic teaching objective, not a universal business preference or a conversion of all consequences into money. It allows known doomed jobs to remain unreleased. If the real decision maker does not know future routes or final quality labels, this perfect-information optimum is a scenario benchmark, not an implementable online policy.

For each reachable phase tuple at time t, retain the smallest accumulated WIP area and a witness sequence. Let J_t(s) be that least area. Initialize J₀(0,…,0)=0. An action from s to s′ adds w(s,u), the number of items inside throughout that slot: previous unfinished WIP plus newly released jobs. A job finishing at the slot end contributes for the whole slot. Update J_(t+1)(s′)=min[J_t(s)+w(s,u)] over predecessors. The final objective uses the endpoint account and this minimum area.

Prove the recurrence by induction. Every feasible t+1-slot history has a feasible t-slot prefix and one allowed last action. Conversely, appending an allowed action to a feasible prefix gives a feasible history. Histories at the same time and phase tuple have the same possible future actions, because our state retains every modeled scheduling dependency. A prefix with larger area cannot improve any future endpoint account and adds the same future area, so it can be discarded. This dominance argument fails if unrecorded waiting age affects quality or if setup state is omitted.

The original four-slot case has job 0=ABAB accepted, job 1=AB accepted, job 2=ABAB scrapped, both machines always available and capacities (1,1). A feasible schedule, writing (A job,B job) and − for idle, is (0,−),(1,0),(0,1),(−,0). It yields phase tuple (4,2,0), two accepted items, no scrap or final WIP, and three visits per machine. Job 0 spends four slots inside and job 1 two, so area is six. Only two jobs are capable of acceptance, proving the output upper bound two. Their six operation durations give an area lower bound six; the schedule attains both. This is an exact optimality argument in addition to the enumerator.

Compare the feasible busier schedule (0,−),(1,0),(2,1),(0,2). It performs seven operations instead of six but finishes only job 1. Jobs 0 and 2 remain unfinished and area is eight. Local activity has consumed the last useful B opportunity without completing the long accepted job. With K_A=0,K_B=1 the optimal accepted count is only one; increasing K_B from one to two when K_A=1 does not improve this four-slot optimum. Neither result establishes a universal buffer investment rule.

**Work:** reproduce every boundary state and compare both schedules. Reuse [Review discrete arguments counting and preserved structure](../../mathematics/core/Review-discrete-arguments-counting-and-preserved-structure.lingua#r_002045D0F1E9276EAC8563DADF) and [Investigate concurrent updates waiting and independent state evidence](../../computer-science/core/Investigate-concurrent-updates-waiting-and-independent-state-evidence.lingua#r_0003B96BB09E60CBDBD0789C4F) for enumeration, invariants and the distinction between possible histories and a scheduler. Explain why increasing capacity cannot reduce the optimized primary output for fixed jobs/calendar/objective: every old feasible schedule remains feasible. Explain why this set-inclusion proof does not predict a fixed heuristic's behavior or a quality process changed by longer waiting.

